 14 │ 
 15 │ A covering index is an index that contains *all* the columns needed to satisfy a query. When MySQL can use a covering index, it doesn't need to access the actual underlying data rows of the table itself.  Instead, all the necessary information is retrieved directly from the index. This avoids a full table scan or, worse, a row-by-row retrieval operation.
    · ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ·                                                                                                                                                                                   ╰── machine-favored phrasing peaks at 'row retrieval operation.'
 16 │ 
    ╰────

dms.machine

  ⚠ machine-favored phrasing peaks at 'columns used in the'
    ╭─[corpus/llm/forum/0720879ca70251ed.md:19:1]
 18 │ 
 19 │ MySQL’s query optimizer analyzes your query and checks if it can satisfy the entire request solely through an existing index.  If the index contains all the columns used in the `SELECT`, `WHERE`, `ORDER BY`, and `GROUP BY` clauses, then a “covering index” is formed – and MySQL will try to use it.
    · ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ·                                                                                         ╰── machine-favored phrasing peaks at 'columns used in the'
